Problem: During the World Cup, there are \( n \) different Panini stickers to collect. Marco's friends are trying to complete their collection, but nobody has a full set of stickers yet! A pair of his friends are said to be wholesome if their combined collection has at least one of each sticker. Marco knows the contents of everyone's collections, and wants to take them all to a restaurant for his birthday. However, he doesn't want any wholesome pairs sitting at the same table.

(i) Show that Marco might need to reserve at least \( n \) different tables.

(ii) Show that \( n \) tables will always be enough for Marco to achieve his goal.

Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}.<|im_end|>
<|im_start|>assistant


Completion:
<think>
Okay, so I have this problem about Marco and his friends trying to collect Panini stickers. The problem has two parts: first, showing that Marco might need at least n different tables, and second, proving that n tables are always enough. Let me try to work through this step by step.

Starting with part (i): Show that Marco might need to reserve at least n different tables. 

So, the key here is that a "wholesome pair" is defined as two friends whose combined collections have at least one of each sticker. Marco doesn't want any wholesome pairs sitting at the same table. Therefore, he needs to arrange the friends so that no two friends who are wholesome pairs are at the same table. The question is, in the worst case, how many tables might he need? And it says "might need at least n", so there exists a scenario where n tables are required.

Hmm. Let me think about how to construct such a scenario. Maybe if each friend is missing a unique sticker? Wait, if each friend is missing a different sticker, then any pair of friends would have combined collections missing the stickers that each is missing. Wait, but if each friend is missing a unique sticker, then two friends would together be missing two different stickers, so their combined collection would be missing two stickers. Therefore, they wouldn't be wholesome pairs. Wait, that would mean that no pair is wholesome? That can't be. Wait, but if each person is missing a unique sticker, then their combined collection would have all stickers except the ones they each are missing. Since each is missing a different one, then combined they have all except two. Therefore, their combined collection is missing two stickers. Hence, they are not wholesome. Therefore, in this case, there are no wholesome pairs. Therefore, Marco can put all the friends at one table. But that contradicts the statement that he might need n tables. So maybe my reasoning is wrong here.

Alternatively, maybe I need a different setup where there are many wholesome pairs, and arranging them such that no two wholesome pairs are together. Maybe the problem is related to graph coloring? Because if we think of each friend as a vertex, and an edge between two vertices if they are a wholesome pair. Then Marco needs to color the vertices with colors (tables) such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color. The minimum number of tables needed would be the chromatic number of the graph. Therefore, the question is asking for a scenario where the chromatic number is at least n.

But how can we have a graph with chromatic number n? For example, if the graph is a complete graph, then the chromatic number is n, but in that case, every pair is a wholesome pair. Therefore, you need n tables. But in that case, how can the graph be a complete graph? That would mean that every pair of friends is a wholesome pair. Which would mean that any two friends combined have all stickers. So that would require that each friend has all stickers except maybe some? Wait, no. If two friends together have all stickers, then each friend individually must have at least all the stickers except maybe some. Wait, no, if two friends together have all stickers, then each one could be missing some stickers, but together they cover all. So if we have a scenario where each pair of friends is a wholesome pair, that would mean that every pair has all the stickers. Therefore, each individual friend must have at least all the stickers except maybe one? Wait, if every pair has all stickers, then each friend must have at least all stickers except maybe a subset of stickers that is not covered by any other friend. But if every pair covers all, then for any sticker, if one person is missing it, then the other person must have it. Therefore, each person is missing at most one sticker. Wait, but if each person is missing a unique sticker, then two people missing different stickers would have together all stickers. Therefore, in that case, every pair is a wholesome pair. Therefore, in this case, the graph is a complete graph, because every pair is connected. Therefore, the chromatic number is n. Therefore, Marco needs n tables. 

Wait, but earlier when I thought of each person missing a unique sticker, that would make the graph complete. So then the chromatic number is n. Therefore, Marco might need n tables. So that is an example where he needs n tables. Therefore, part (i) is shown. 

But let me check again. Suppose there are n friends, each missing a unique sticker. Then, any two friends together have all stickers, since each is missing a different one. Therefore, any two friends are wholesome. Therefore, the graph is complete, so chromatic number is n. Therefore, Marco must reserve n tables, each with one person. Because if you try to put two people at the same table, they are a wholesome pair and that's not allowed. Therefore, you need n tables
